# Markdown pages

This wiki can store pages in **Markdown** as well as in the native
[[HelpOnMoinWikiSyntax|MoinMoin wiki syntax]]. Both kinds of pages can be
used side by side: they link to each other, they appear in searches,
RecentChanges and backlinks, and they have history, diffs and attachments.

<<TableOfContents(2)>>

## Writing a Markdown page

Just write the page in Markdown: MoinMoin reads the text and recognizes by
itself whether a page is written in Markdown or in MoinMoin wiki markup
(e.g. from `# Title` headings, `**bold**`, `[text](link)` links, lists
starting at the beginning of the line, ```` ``` ```` code blocks). Existing
wiki pages stay as they are.

If a page is too short or too plain to be recognized (it is then shown as
wiki markup), put this line at the very top of the page (it is not shown):

```
#format markdown
```

or create the page from the [[MarkdownTemplate]]. A `#format` line always
wins over the automatic recognition (`#format moin` forces MoinMoin wiki markup;
the old `#format wiki` works too).
The wiki administrator can turn the recognition off with
`markup_autodetect = False`, and make Markdown the format of the pages
without a `#format` line with `default_markup = 'markdown'`.

Lines like `# Title` and `## Section` in a Markdown page are Markdown
headings (the MoinMoin processing instructions, like `#acl`, have no blank
after the `#`).

## Text

| Markdown | Result |
|----------|--------|
| `**bold**` | **bold** |
| `*italic*` | *italic* |
| `` `code` `` | `code` |
| `~~~` / ```` ``` ```` blocks | code blocks |
| `> quote` | quotation |
| `* item` or `1. item` | lists |
| `---` | horizontal rule |

Tables, footnotes[^note], definition lists and abbreviations are
supported, too.

[^note]: Like this footnote.

## Links

* `[[PageName]]` and `[[PageName|text]]`, like in wiki markup: [[FrontPage]]
* `[text](PageName)`: [the front page](FrontPage)
* `[text](/SubPage)` and `[text](../SisterPage)`: relative page names
* `[[PageName#anchor|text]]`: a section of a page
* `[[MoinMoin:HelpOnMacros]]`: interwiki links
* `[text](https://example.org)` and `<https://example.org>`: external links

Link targets without a URL scheme are wiki pages. For security, only
safe URL schemes (http, https, ftp, mailto, ...) are allowed.

## Attachments and images

* `[[attachment:file.pdf|text]]` or `[text](attachment:file.pdf)`
* `![description](attachment:image.png)` shows an attached image
* `![description](https://example.org/image.png)` shows an external image

## Macros

MoinMoin macros work like in wiki markup, e.g. `<<TableOfContents>>`,
`<<RecentChanges>>`, `<<FullSearch(text)>>` or `<<Date(...)>>`.
Inside `` `code` `` they are not executed.

## Code and other markups

A fenced block with a language name is highlighted:

```python
def hello(name):
    return "Hello, %s!" % name
```

A fenced block called `wiki` (or `creole`, `rst`, `csv`) is rendered
with that markup, so you can use MoinMoin wiki syntax inside a Markdown
page:

```wiki
''MoinMoin'' '''wiki''' markup, with a <<Icon(smile.png)>> smiley icon.
```

## Not supported

Raw HTML is shown as text, it is not rendered (the wiki stays safe from
scripts). The graphical editor only works for wiki markup pages, Markdown
pages are edited with the text editor.
